{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.2.d2b9e01465","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":2,"group":"bnd-ne-74","passage":"<p>The documentary's narrator—a retired ______ what Earth looks like from orbit.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["astronaut, describes","astronaut—describes","astronaut; describes","astronaut describes"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The phrase “a retired astronaut” is extra information about “the documentary's narrator.” It opens with a dash, so it must close with the same punctuation: a pair of <b>dashes</b>. Mixing punctuation marks or leaving the element unclosed is incorrect."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.2.e4113b7457","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":2,"group":"bnd-ne-82","passage":"<p>The spacecraft, launched more than forty years ______ still sending data back to Earth.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["ago; is","ago—is","ago, is","ago is"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The phrase “launched more than forty years ago” is extra information about “the spacecraft.” It opens with a comma, so it must close with the same punctuation: a pair of <b>commas</b>. Mixing punctuation marks or leaving the element unclosed is incorrect."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.2.6624a85a76","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":2,"group":"bnd-ne-88","passage":"<p>The owl—a species that hunts mainly at ______ in hollow trees.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["dusk nests","dusk; nests","dusk, nests","dusk—nests"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The phrase “a species that hunts mainly at dusk” is extra information about “the owl.” It opens with a dash, so it must close with the same punctuation: a pair of <b>dashes</b>. Mixing punctuation marks or leaving the element unclosed is incorrect."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.2.2ffdd3e89f","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":2,"group":"bnd-ne-89","passage":"<p>The old mill—converted into apartments five years ______ the river.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["ago—overlooks","ago; overlooks","ago overlooks","ago, overlooks"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The phrase “converted into apartments five years ago” is extra information about “the old mill.” It opens with a dash, so it must close with the same punctuation: a pair of <b>dashes</b>. Mixing punctuation marks or leaving the element unclosed is incorrect."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.2.fea3b6cafd","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":2,"group":"bnd-colon-20","passage":"<p>Her research focused on a single ______ some birds migrate at night.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["question why","question, and why","question: why","question; why"],"answer":"C","explanation":"“Her research focused on a single question” is a complete independent clause, and what follows is a phrase that explains or identifies something in it. A <b>colon</b> correctly introduces that explanation. A semicolon needs an independent clause on both sides, and “and” wrongly joins a clause to a phrase."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.2.a2c135be51","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":2,"group":"bnd-colon-23","passage":"<p>The storm left behind a clear ______ power of nature.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["reminder, and the","reminder; the","reminder the","reminder: the"],"answer":"D","explanation":"“The storm left behind a clear reminder” is a complete independent clause, and what follows is a phrase that explains or identifies something in it. A <b>colon</b> correctly introduces that explanation. A semicolon needs an independent clause on both sides, and “and” wrongly joins a clause to a phrase."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.2.a4c0a93064","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":2,"group":"bnd-colon-38","passage":"<p>The toolkit contains everything needed for basic ______ hammer, screwdrivers, pliers, and a tape measure.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["repairs; a","repairs: a","repairs a","repairs, a"],"answer":"B","explanation":"“The toolkit contains everything needed for basic repairs” is a complete independent clause, and what follows is a list that specifies it. A <b>colon</b> correctly introduces such a list. A semicolon must join two independent clauses, and a comma or no punctuation fails to set off the list clearly."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.2.2802c08ae8","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":2,"group":"bnd-colon-41","passage":"<p>The inspectors found the source of the ______ cracked pipe beneath the kitchen floor.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["leak, and a","leak a","leak: a","leak; a"],"answer":"C","explanation":"“The inspectors found the source of the leak” is a complete independent clause, and what follows is a phrase that explains or identifies something in it. A <b>colon</b> correctly introduces that explanation. A semicolon needs an independent clause on both sides, and “and” wrongly joins a clause to a phrase."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.2.e0b854b066","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":2,"group":"bnd-colon-45","passage":"<p>The engineers identified the cause of the ______ single faulty sensor.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["failure: a","failure, and a","failure; a","failure a"],"answer":"A","explanation":"“The engineers identified the cause of the failure” is a complete independent clause, and what follows is a phrase that explains or identifies something in it. A <b>colon</b> correctly introduces that explanation. A semicolon needs an independent clause on both sides, and “and” wrongly joins a clause to a phrase."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.2.ee5347341d","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":2,"group":"bnd-colon-57","passage":"<p>The camp teaches campers several outdoor ______ building, map reading, and first aid.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["skills fire","skills; fire","skills: fire","skills, fire"],"answer":"C","explanation":"“The camp teaches campers several outdoor skills” is a complete independent clause, and what follows is a list that specifies it. A <b>colon</b> correctly introduces such a list. A semicolon must join two independent clauses, and a comma or no punctuation fails to set off the list clearly."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.2.4abb14d431","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":2,"group":"bnd-iq-13","passage":"<p>Readers often ask why the novel ends so ______ author has never explained her choice.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["abruptly? The","abruptly. The","abruptly, the","abruptly? the"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The first sentence reports a question indirectly (for example, “wondered why” or “asked whether”) instead of asking it directly, so it ends with a <b>period</b>, not a question mark. A comma would create a comma splice."}
